For $\lambda>0$, let ${\Delta_c}={\Delta_c}(\lambda)$. The function ${\Psi}$ defined in (14.1) is a 
$(s, \delta, c)$-potential function such that 
{
\begin{align}
s^{-1}&= 1-{\textstyle \frac{{\Delta_c}-1}{2}}\log\left(1+\textstyle{\frac{1}{{\Delta_c}-1}} \right) \enspace, 
&\delta& \leq {\textstyle \frac{1}{{\Delta_c}}} &\textrm{and} && c \leq {\textstyle \frac{\lambda}{1+\lambda} }\enspace.
\end{align}
}

Let ${\Delta} > 1$ and $k,N\geq 1$ be integers, $c>0$, $\uprho> 1$, $s\geq 1$ while 
let $b,\varepsilon\in (0,1)$. Also, let $\beta,\gamma, \lambda\in \mathbb{R}$ be such that $\gamma >0$,
$0\leq \beta\leq \gamma$ and $\lambda>0$. 


Consider graph $G=(V,E)$ of maximum degree ${\Delta}$ such that 

$\|  (\UpH_{G,k})^N\|^{1/N}_{2}= \uprho$.

Assume that $\mu_G$, the Gibbs distribution on $G$ specified by the parameters $(\beta, \gamma, \lambda)$,
is $b$-marginally bounded.

For $\delta= \frac{1-\varepsilon}{\uprho}$, suppose that there
is a $(s,\delta,c)$-potential function ${\Psi}$ with respect to $(\beta,\gamma,\lambda)$. 


There exists a fixed constant $\widehat{c} \geq 1$ such that 
for any $\Lambda\subset V$ and $\tau\in \{\pm 1\}^{\Lambda}$, the extended influence matrix 
${\mathcal{L}}^{\Lambda,\tau}_{G,k}$, that is induced by $\mu^{\Lambda,\tau}_G$, satisfies that
\begin{align}
\|   | {\mathcal{L}}^{\Lambda,\tau}_{G,k}| \|_{2s} &\leq \widehat{c}\cdot \sum\nolimits_{\ell\geq k} 
\left(
 {\textstyle \left(\frac{1-\varepsilon/2}{\uprho}\right)^{\ell} } \cdot 

\|  ( \UpH_{G,k})^{\ell} \|_{2} 
 \right)^{1/s}
\enspace.
\end{align}

There exists fixed constant $\widehat{c}_1>0$ such that 
for any ${\bf q} \in \mathbb{R}^{{\mathcal{E}}_{\Lambda,k}}_{\geq 0}$, we have
\begin{align}
\upf^{s}_{{\bf q}, \ell} \leq \widehat{c}_1\cdot (1+\varepsilon/3)^{\ell} \cdot \upz^{s}_{{\bf q},\ell} \enspace,
\end{align}
where the inequality between the two vectors is entry-wise.
